  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$",
  "worked_solution": "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}^{\u2032}-(x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x){(x)}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)-(x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
